# macOS authentication setup recovery

Scope: native macOS Host authentication and authenticated display bootstrap,
branch macos-auth-recovery. No global display-power policy or Linux Host changes.

Observed failure
- Read-only live allocation ownership inspection found 16 authentication
  records retained by the setup-token dictionary, with no stream lease.
  No token content or private deployment information was collected into Git.
- A failed topology GET retained its authenticated token. Automatic reconnect
  authenticated again, eventually filling the old 16-token table and making
  subsequent auth/start return busy before password verification.

Implementation
- Keep 16 short-lived pending challenges as the unauthenticated resource bound.
- Keep at most four unused verified setup tokens, at most one per verified
  account (UID and UUID) and network peer. Authenticate before superseding;
  invalid passwords/names and different users sharing NAT cannot revoke access.
- Permit challenge/verification even when setup capacity is full, so a verified
  owner can replace their own abandoned token. Preserve bounded verification.
- Revoke authorized setup on topology failure/cancellation and delivery error.
  Preserve a successful token for display preparation/launch.
- Keep the live lease separate. Login does not grant implicit takeover or
  disturb a working stream. Unobserved abandonment retains finite expiry.

Qualification
- Synthetic real session class: 64 replacement cycles, full-table replacement,
  wrong credentials/owner, peer isolation, same-peer different-account isolation,
  stream-lease survival, expiry, revocation and blocked-verifier scope change.
- Real loopback TLS with synthetic account/display: 32 consecutive failed
  topology setups without expiry, each token revoked; recovery success,
  scope revocation and request timeout.
- Run those fixtures in every macOS Host build, plus existing packaging gates.
- Build/package on GitHub; no production restart/install without operator
  coordination. Live reconnect acceptance remains required after installation.

Limits
- Bounded admission may still reject concurrent overload or invalid credentials;
  this is not a promise of availability through arbitrary OS/network failures.
- Display wake/recovery is a separate issue. Removing auth exhaustion must not
  be presented as proof that an inactive capture display can always recover.

Follow-up: precise failures before lifecycle simplification
- Separate helper unavailability from rejected credentials using existing busy
  state; do not remove cooldown, signature, ownership or private IPC checks.
- Return only a bounded stage enum from the isolated directory verifier. Log
  fixed stage/result/elapsed time in the parent, suppressing repeated identical
  diagnostics for five seconds. Never emit directory error descriptions,
  account names/IDs, passwords, tokens or peer addresses.
- Exercise real isolated helper framing/timeout/cleanup on hosted builders,
  alongside session and topology fixtures. This is not live OD validation.
- Determine the remaining live failure before redesigning desktop preparation.
  Target authenticate once -> prepare desktop -> stream; readiness retry must
  not repeatedly verify credentials or accumulate setup tokens. Authority
  changes, cancellation and takeover still need explicit state/ownership tests.
- Live diagnostics identified retry-cooldown during ongoing reconnect attempts,
  not a directory rejection. Clear backoff after verified credentials, retain
  it after failures; test immediate success/success and denied/backoff sequences.
  This avoids penalizing successful authentication without permitting parallel
  directory work or removing failed-password throttling.
- With the retrying Client stopped, real PLANK authentication succeeds. The
  topology request then fails HTTP503 (Qt's displayed network error403 is not
  HTTP403). A fresh worker has no owned virtual display yet; its inactive
  current desktop cannot provide topology, while recovery required an owned
  display. Add bounded authenticated wake-only bootstrap with no physical-mode
  mutation or guessed resolution. Keep existing owned-mode recovery and test
  both paths, including cancellation, failed wake and unavailable display.
- Live wake succeeds before capture geometry leaves its reconfiguration phase.
  Wait at most one additional second on the existing authorized setup lane,
  bounded by the original request deadline. Never reauthenticate while waiting.
  TLS fixtures cover transient and permanent geometry unavailability.

Dynamic Match Client follow-up
- Retain manual presets and Linux EDID restrictions. Mac display preparation
  already carries numeric width/height in schema 2; accept even native-pixel
  dimensions 2–8192 on each axis, without nearest-preset substitution.
- Use the same CoreGraphics native-pixel query in Mac Client authentication
  and streaming. Do not confuse Retina logical/backing sizes with native pixels,
  or assume SDL and CoreGraphics enumeration order is identical.
- Keep one custom 60 Hz virtual mode alongside presets. Register new custom
  modes only on an online existing output; preserve bounded ownership checks.
- Test laptop/portrait dimensions, changed reconnect sizes, invalid dimensions,
  delayed output readiness and Linux preset rejection. Hosted checks do not
  replace real laptop/encoder acceptance. Build matching Host and Mac Client.

Secure-unlock user activity
- Observed LoginWindow waits five seconds for user activity, then reports an
  incorrect password without the user becoming active. Separate account
  verification succeeds. A bounded caffeinate -u test restores normal unlock;
  this is not evidence of a Shift/keymap or credential failure.
- Report console UserIsActive using public IOKit APIs only after a validated
  client input event passes the existing lease, topology and permission checks.
  This intentionally wakes the console for interactive use, including an
  already-active virtual display; remote/network-only activity is not enough.
- Rate-limit to one request per second and set a ten-second timeout. Idle
  connections, discovery, media frames, server key repeats and teardown
  releases must not renew activity. Stop releases the session-owned assertion;
  deallocation is a cleanup fallback. No global sleep policy, new service,
  permission prompt, credential logging or authentication bypass.
- Test using non-waking IOKit replacements: rate limit, expiry/re-arm, terminal
  stop, deallocation, assertion/property failures. Exercise native input with
  real loopback QUIC and a non-posting sink for revoked ownership, permissions,
  invalid events, synthetic repeats and disconnect. Run in Host package builds.
- Live acceptance: idle locked Host, normal typing unlock, disconnect/reconnect,
  and assertion release. Synthetic tests are not proof of live secure unlock.

Validation result
- Hosted signed run 35057106376 passed from b7407097fef78f10952f9969d6711d92ed454890.
  Authentication fixture: 504 checks; repeated real-TLS topology failures:
  32 attempts; recovery success/failure/revocation/timeout all pass.
  All existing package gates, signing and notarization pass.
- Follow-up Host 1.0.111 passed 23 synthetic display cases, six TLS recovery
  scenarios and 509 authentication checks. Installed signed payload was verified.
  Real authenticated topology and exact 3024x1964, 3456x2234, 2880x1864 and
  5120x2160 preparation passed; initial dimensions were restored afterward.
- Matching Mac Client run 35062445761 passed all 16 shared topology tests and
  signed/notarized DMG gates. The earlier run compiled the app but failed the
  new standalone test's missing SDK27 include; build policy now supplies it.
- Laptop presentation/reconnect acceptance and full Client retry-lifecycle
  simplification remain outstanding. No merge; see HANDOFF for exact provenance.
